GlitchVape for Debian
=====================

Three binary packages come out of this source, and one of them is not in
main. That is the only thing about this packaging that needs explaining.

  glitchvape              the library and the command-line tools
  glitchvape-gui          the Gtk3 window
  glitchvape-fonts-extra  one typeface, non-free/fonts

Why the third package exists
----------------------------

GlitchVape asks for fonts by *role* -- vcr, pixel, ui, cjk -- rather than by
name, and falls through a list of candidates per role to whatever fontconfig
can see. So a missing font is a different-looking render, never an error.

Three of the fonts it wants ship in the base package because their terms are
known and permit it. W95FA does not: dafont describes it as SIL Open Font
License and free for personal and commercial use, but the download carries no
licence text and no statement by its author has been found that can be cited.
That is a claim about the terms rather than the terms, and it is not enough
for a package that says "MIT and OFL-1.1" on the tin.

Rather than drop the font or overstate what is known about it, it is packaged
on its own, so that installing it is a decision somebody makes rather than
one made for them.

What an upload to Debian proper would need
------------------------------------------

A source package in main may not contain non-free content, and this one does:
assets/fonts-nonfree/ is in the upstream tarball. So this source cannot go to
main as it stands, whatever the Section fields on the individual binaries say.
Built locally, or in a PPA, or in any repository that does not enforce archive
areas, it works exactly as intended.

Two ways forward, in order of preference:

  1. Settle the licence. If W95FA's author says publicly what its terms are,
     or the upstream release turns up with its OFL text, the font moves from
     assets/fonts-nonfree/ to assets/fonts/ and this package disappears --
     `make check-licenses` promotes it without anyone editing a list. This is
     how VCR OSD Mono stopped being a question: its author was asked, and
     answered.

  2. Repack and split. debian/copyright carries a commented-out
     Files-Excluded line that strips assets/fonts-nonfree from the tarball;
     uncommenting it makes this source main-suitable and drops the third
     binary package. The font would then need a source package of its own in
     non-free, which is a separate piece of work and not worth doing while
     option 1 is open.

Where the fonts end up
----------------------

  /usr/share/glitchvape/assets/fonts/           base package
  /usr/share/glitchvape/assets/fonts-nonfree/   glitchvape-fonts-extra
  /usr/share/glitchvape/fonts/                  yours, system-wide
  ~/.local/share/glitchvape/fonts/              yours, per-account

The last two are searched first and are never touched by an upgrade, so a
newer release of any bundled font can be dropped in without being fought over.
`glitchvape --check-fonts` prints which file each role resolved to and names
the directory to put a missing one in.

